---
registryId: pgvector
---

{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is pgvector?

pgvector is an open-source PostgreSQL extension that adds vector data types, distance
operators, exact nearest-neighbor search, and HNSW or IVFFlat approximate indexes. It
lets an application store embeddings beside relational identifiers, tenant ownership,
permissions, timestamps, source versions, and ordinary searchable columns.

An embedding is a numeric representation produced by a specific model and preprocessing
contract. Nearby vectors may represent semantically similar inputs, but distance is not
a universal meaning: the model, normalization, distance function, data distribution,
and evaluation set determine whether a neighbor is useful.

The core invariant is: **compare only compatible embeddings and measure approximate
results against exact search**. PostgreSQL transactions and recovery protect stored
rows; they do not make mixed embedding models comparable or guarantee that an
approximate, filtered query returns enough relevant results.
{% /section-card %}

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Vector value" eyebrow="Representation" icon="braces" tone="blue" %}
`vector` stores single-precision values, while `halfvec`, `bit`, and `sparsevec` support
other size and similarity trade-offs in current pgvector releases.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Distance operator" eyebrow="Ranking" icon="route" tone="cyan" %}
Use the operator class that matches L2, inner product, cosine, L1, Hamming, or Jaccard
semantics and the stored representation.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Approximate index" eyebrow="Candidate retrieval" icon="layers" tone="violet" %}
HNSW and IVFFlat reduce search work by examining a candidate subset, trading recall,
memory, build cost, and write maintenance for speed.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Relational predicate" eyebrow="Business scope" icon="filter" tone="green" %}
Tenant, authorization, category, freshness, model version, and lifecycle filters remain
ordinary PostgreSQL data contracts around the vector search.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Build one versioned search row

Do not store a vector without the evidence needed to decide whether it is searchable.
At minimum, retain the source object's stable identity, tenant or policy scope,
embedding version, content version, timestamps, and the original or reconstructable
text reference.

{% system-flow title="One embedding publication" caption="The vector becomes searchable only after source identity, model compatibility, policy scope, and database commit agree." %}
{% system-node title="Source object" eyebrow="Business truth" icon="file" tone="blue" %}
Identify the document, chunk, image, product, or event and the exact source version that
produced the representation.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Embedding contract" eyebrow="Model truth" icon="cpu" tone="violet" %}
Record model, preprocessing, dimensions, normalization, tokenizer or chunker, and
generation timestamp.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="PostgreSQL row" eyebrow="Transactional state" icon="database" tone="cyan" %}
Write relational metadata and vector under one transaction or an explicit staged
publication protocol.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Search policy" eyebrow="Read boundary" icon="shield" tone="green" %}
Filter by tenant, authorization, lifecycle, freshness, and compatible embedding version
before treating neighbors as candidates.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Keep model rollouts reversible

- Add a new embedding version beside the old one or in a separately addressable table
  or partition.
- Backfill from stable source versions and track coverage, failures, and stale sources.
- Evaluate old and new versions on the same labeled and production-sampled queries.
- Switch read policy only after quality, latency, coverage, and rollback gates pass.
- Remove old embeddings only after every reader and recovery path stops referencing
  them.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Start with exact search as the correctness baseline

Without an approximate index, PostgreSQL can order compatible rows by distance and
return exact nearest neighbors. Exact search is often sufficient for small or strongly
filtered sets and remains the reference used to measure ANN recall.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="L2 distance" eyebrow="<->" icon="move" tone="blue" %}
Use Euclidean distance when the model and evaluation contract interpret geometric
distance directly.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Cosine distance" eyebrow="<=>" icon="circle" tone="cyan" %}
Use cosine distance for directional similarity. Zero vectors are not indexed for cosine
distance and normalization choices must stay consistent.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Negative inner product" eyebrow="<#>" icon="minus" tone="violet" %}
Use inner product when the embedding model supports it. For unit-normalized vectors,
the pgvector guide notes inner product can be fastest for exact search.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Make the planner-visible query shape canonical

- Include `ORDER BY` on the raw distance operator and `LIMIT`; wrapping the expression
  in a different sort expression can prevent the intended index path.
- Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` with representative parameters, cache states, tenant
  sizes, and result counts.
- Keep a controlled exact-search path by disabling index scans inside a transaction for
  offline recall evaluation.
- Measure relevance, result count, latency, rows visited, buffers, temporary I/O, and
  plan changes together.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Choose HNSW or IVFFlat from measured trade-offs

HNSW builds a multilayer graph. It generally offers a stronger speed-recall trade-off
than IVFFlat, but uses more memory and has slower builds and inserts. IVFFlat clusters
vectors into lists and probes selected lists; it builds faster and uses less memory but
needs representative data for training and careful list/probe tuning.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="HNSW" eyebrow="Graph search" icon="share" tone="violet" %}
Tune `m` and `ef_construction` for build shape, then `hnsw.ef_search` for query candidate
breadth. Higher values can improve recall while increasing work.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="IVFFlat" eyebrow="Clustered lists" icon="grid" tone="cyan" %}
Build after the table has representative data. Tune list count during index creation
and probe count per query; too many lists on too little data can harm results.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Exact scan" eyebrow="Ground truth" icon="check" tone="blue" %}
Keep no ANN index for bounded datasets or selective predicates, and use exact results to
measure the quality of every approximate release.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

The lab exposes exact vector payload arithmetic and makes index size and recall explicit
benchmark assumptions. Filter selectivity is modeled separately because pgvector ANN
filters are applied after the index produces candidates.

{% interactive-block id="technology/pgvector-performance" dataFile="/api/content/technology/pgvector/data/index-planner.json" /%}

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/pgvector/code/estimate-vector-footprint.py" language="python" title="Estimate vector payload and filtered candidate demand" /%}

The current [pgvector project guide](https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector#readme)
documents index parameters, supported types, dimension limits, iterative scans, build
progress, and tuning recommendations for the deployed release.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Design filtered nearest-neighbor queries deliberately

With an approximate index, PostgreSQL scans vector candidates and then applies ordinary
filters. If a tenant or category matches 5% of rows and the initial scan visits 40
candidates, only about two matching rows are expected before iterative expansion.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Apply exact predicates" label="Business scope" %}
Filter tenant, authorization, model version, lifecycle, and other mandatory conditions
inside the search query.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Retrieve candidates" label="ANN budget" %}
Use HNSW `ef_search` or IVFFlat probes to control initial work. The two parameters have
different meanings and need separate benchmarks.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Expand if underfilled" label="Iterative scan" %}
Enable strict or relaxed iterative scans and set bounded scan limits when post-filtering
does not produce enough rows.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Rerank and gate" label="Final decision" %}
Optionally rerank a bounded candidate set with full vectors, lexical features, or a
cross-encoder, then apply thresholds and policy before returning results.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

### Match relational indexing to selectivity

- Add B-tree, GIN, GiST, BRIN, hash, or multicolumn indexes for exact predicates when
  they improve the complete plan.
- Use a partial vector index for a few stable values with enough data per index.
- Consider table partitioning when many stable values need independent placement and
  lifecycle, but include partition count and cross-partition search cost.
- Measure underfilled result sets as a correctness failure, not only a latency metric.
- Bound iterative scan tuples, probes, memory, total statement time, and result count.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Observe model, replica, and filtered-search failure

pgvector inherits PostgreSQL WAL, replication, backup, point-in-time recovery, MVCC,
roles, and row-level policy. That is useful operational leverage, but a lagging replica
can still serve an older search universe and a mixed-model table can still rank
incompatible vectors.

{% interactive-block id="technology/pgvector-correctness-lab" dataFile="/api/content/technology/pgvector/data/correctness-incidents.json" /%}

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/pgvector/code/production-search.sql" language="sql" title="Create a versioned HNSW search path with bounded iterative scans" /%}

### Rehearse the complete failure path

- Query while a new embedding version is partially backfilled and verify version
  predicates prevent mixed-space ranking.
- Pause replica replay, write new and deleted content, and verify freshness-sensitive
  search routes or rejects according to the lag budget.
- Add a selective tenant/category filter and confirm iterative scan returns enough rows
  without violating statement-time or scan limits.
- Kill an index build, cancel a concurrent rebuild, and observe locks, WAL, disk,
  maintenance memory, and the old index's availability.
- Restore PostgreSQL to a point in time and verify vectors, relational metadata, source
  versions, and application caches agree.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Load, build, and maintain indexes as PostgreSQL state

Vector columns and indexes participate in ordinary database storage, WAL, vacuum,
replication, and planner behavior. Large ANN indexes can increase memory pressure, index
build time, WAL volume, replica lag, backups, and maintenance windows.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric label="Payload" value="rows x (bytes/dimension + header)" detail="vector uses 4 bytes/dimension; halfvec uses 2" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric label="Index" value="measured graph or list bytes" detail="Record build parameters and data version" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric label="Build" value="time + memory + WAL + locks" detail="Use concurrent builds where write availability requires them" tone="amber" /%}
{% metric label="Query" value="latency + recall + rows + buffers" detail="Slice by filter, tenant, model version, and cache state" tone="cyan" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

### Keep ingestion and maintenance bounded

- Use binary `COPY` or measured bulk inserts for initial loads and build ANN indexes
  after representative data is present.
- Create production replacement indexes concurrently and preserve enough disk, WAL,
  memory, and time for both old and new copies.
- Size `maintenance_work_mem` from host and concurrent maintenance capacity; HNSW build
  speed changes when the graph no longer fits.
- Track dead tuples, autovacuum, analyze, table and index bloat, checkpoints, WAL,
  replica lag, and index usage.
- Reindex and vacuum HNSW according to measured maintenance behavior and the deployed
  pgvector/PostgreSQL versions.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Secure and operate search as a relational data product

Keeping vectors beside relational policy enables one transactional and authorization
boundary, but only if every query includes that boundary and database roles cannot
bypass it unintentionally.

### Production review checklist

- Enforce tenant and authorization filters in reviewed SQL, views, row-level security,
  or a tested combination that applies before results leave PostgreSQL.
- Do not expose raw embeddings, source text, distances, or neighbor identities across
  tenant boundaries through logs, traces, caches, or debugging endpoints.
- Version extension, PostgreSQL, client, embedding model, schema, index parameters, and
  query policy together.
- Monitor p50/p95/p99 latency, statement timeout, rows visited, buffers, index usage,
  exact-vs-ANN recall, underfilled queries, empty queries, and relevance feedback.
- Track insert/update/delete rate, backfill coverage, stale embeddings, index growth,
  WAL, backup duration, replica lag, vacuum, and rebuild progress.
- Load-test primary and replica failure, failover, cache cold start, index rebuild,
  selective filters, hot tenants, and model rollback.

The design question is not “Can PostgreSQL store embeddings?” It is “Can one database
preserve model compatibility, relational policy, recoverability, and measured search
quality as the vector working set and query mix change?”
{% /section-card %}

